1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f4!? { Hello and welcome to the fifteen minutes video about the Najdorf
with 6.f4! Lots of lines to cover, I tried to briefly check all critical lines, but of course, the goal of our setup is to be as aggressive
and principled as possible, so it is worth checking out all chapters in bigger detail. } { [%csl Bf4] } 6... e5 (6... e6 { Second most common and very playable
line. } 7. Qf3!? { My recommendation, we simply want to develop the queenside, and keep the g4 resource in reserve. } { [%cal Gc1e3,Ge1c1,Bg2g4] } 7... Qb6 { Main line. } (7... Qc7?! { Popular reply, with 750 games, but quite dubious. } 8. g4! { The inclusion is definitely in
White's favor. } { [%csl Yc7,Gf3] } 8... Nc6 9. Nxc6! { We must play concretely. } { [%csl Gc6] } 9... bxc6 { [%cal Gd6d5] } 10. g5 Nd7 11. Bd2!? { I
like this semi-prophylactic move. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gd2c3] } 11... d5 12. h4 { Bb4 makes no sense now. } { [%cal Rf8b4] } 12... d4!? $146 { Engine's recommendation. } 13. Na4 c5 14. b3! $16 { Typical defensive setup, and White has a safe positional advantage. } { [%csl Gb3][%cal Ga4b2,Gh4h5] }) 8. a3!? { Overall useful move, preventing any b5-b4 ideas, and of course, Black cannot just grab the knight here. } { [%csl Ba3][%cal Yb7b5,Yb5b4,Rb6d4,Gc1e3] } 8... Nc6 { Most
common by far. } (8... Qxd4?? 9. Be3 $18 { [%cal Rd4b4] }) 9. Nxc6! { We take of course. } 9... bxc6 { Necessary. } 10. e5! { That's
another reason why I like 8.a3, in the main line we reach our typical play against Black's structure. } { [%csl Ge5] } 10... Nd7 11. exd6 Bxd6 12. Qg3! $146 { And Black has issues with the kingside. } { [%csl Rg7][%cal Gf1d3,Gc1d2,Re8g8,Gc3e4] }) (6... Qc7 { Third most common move, Black stops e5. } 7. f5!? { Very rare and interesting approach to quickly play with g4. } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bg2g4] } 7... e5 { Most common, to force White to
take. } { [%cal Rf5e6] } 8. fxe6 fxe6 9. Be2!? { Most promising reply. } { [%cal Ge2h5] } 9... Be7 { Black's best. } 10. Bh5+! { White's idea, Black can't
fianchetto the bishop anymore. } { [%cal Rf8g7] } 10... g6 11. Be2 { Now the dark squares on the kingside are forever weak. } { [%csl Yg7,Yh6] }) (6... g6 { Rare option,
but natural one, Black wants to fianchetto the bishop. } 7. e5!? { The most principled, we aim for a good structure. } { [%csl Be5] } 7... dxe5! { Necessary. } 8. fxe5 { Black has two main moves, which were played equally thirty times each: } 8... Ng4 { More direct. } (8... Nd5 { More
positional. } 9. Qf3!? { My recommendation. } { [%csl Bf3] } 9... Nxc3 10. Qxc3 { White's idea, to keep the structure intact. } { [%csl Gc3] } 10... Bg7 11. Bf4 O-O 12. O-O-O { Starting position, and Black has to be accurate to reach a playable position. }) 9. e6! { White's main idea of the
line. } { [%csl Ge6] } 9... f5 { Most common move. } (9... Bxe6! { Best, but it's a bit sad. } 10. Nxe6 Qxd1+ 11. Nxd1 fxe6 { Black is a pawn up for now, but that's
the only good news in the position. }) 10. h3 { to kick the knight back. } 10... Ne5 { Best, to stop Bc4 and support Nbc6 as well. } { [%cal Rf1c4,Gb8c6] } 11. Be3 { The structure is very atypical, but the e6-pawn is an actual strength, making Black's king very
vulnerable, and development wise, White has more natural moves. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Gd1d2,Ge1c1] } 11... Nbc6! { Necessary. } { [%csl Gc6] } (11... Bg7? 12. Nxf5!! { works. } { [%csl Bf5] } 12... Qxd1+ 13. Rxd1 gxf5 14. Nd5 $16 { White will win the exchange. } { [%csl Ra8][%cal Gd5c7] }) 12. Nxc6 { Trading queens. } 12... Qxd1+ 13. Rxd1 bxc6 { Necessary, to guard d5. } { [%csl Gd5] } 14. Na4!? { Most annoying and White keeps the initiative. } { [%cal Ba4b6] }) (6... Nc6 { Rare system,
usually played by grandmasters ready for a fight. } 7. Nxc6 { My recommendation, we transform the structure. } 7... bxc6 8. e5! { The idea is to break Black's chain, leaving the damaged a6-c6 pawns, and we are not scared to do it even at
the cost of a pawn. } { [%csl Ra6,Rc6,Bf8][%cal Rd6e5,Gf4e5] } 8... Nd7! { Necessary. } 9. Bc4 { Most testing, Black has tried a few moves: } 9... Nb6 { Most common and
best, to play d5. } { [%csl Rc4][%cal Gd6d5] } (9... d5? { Trap. } 10. Nxd5! cxd5 11. Qxd5 $18 { Double attack. } { [%csl Ra8,Rf7][%cal Gd5f7,Gd5a8] }) (9... dxe5?! { Quite
bad. } 10. Qf3! $146 { Strongest move, the idea is to castle long fast. } { [%cal Gc1d2,Ge1c1] } 10... e6! { Necessary. } 11. Bd2 Be7 12. O-O-O $14 { Huge development lead in return for one pawn. } { [%csl Gc3,Gc4,Gd2,Gf3] }) 10. Qf3!? $146 { I like this move
order, making sure that d5 will be played. } { [%csl Rc6][%cal Rb6c4,Gf3c6] } 10... d5 11. Bd3 e6 12. O-O $13 { We enter this interesting structure. } { [%csl Yb6][%cal Gd7c5] }) 7. Nb3 Nbd7 { The mainline. } (7... Be7 { This is Black's second most common move and a very logical one. } 8. fxe5! { This is the key way. } 8... dxe5 9. Qxd8+ Bxd8 { Looks unsuspicious, but Black has a tough task here, actually. } 10. Be3 { The most logical move. } { [%csl Be3] } 10... Nbd7 { The most
common move. } (10... Be6 { was recently played by Grandmaster L'Ami. } 11. Nc5! { a very important idea. } { [%csl Bc5,Re6] } 11... b5 12. Nxe6 $146 fxe6 13. a4! { asking immediate questions. In general, you should play this endgame actively with White. } { [%csl Ba4] } 13... b4 14. Na2 { [%csl Rb4] } 14... a5 15. Bb5+ Nbd7 16. Nc1 $16 { Nd3-000 next. White has a stable advantage. } { [%cal Gc1d3,Ge1c1] }) 11. Be2! { very important
to start with this. } { [%csl Be2] } 11... b6 { probably the best, to develop with Bb7. } { [%cal Gc8b7] } 12. O-O-O Bb7 13. Rhf1 $14 { For the engine this is only
+0,40, but over the board it feels much more. }) (7... Qc7 { Very serious try. } 8. Be2! { The best chance. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Ge1g1,Gg2g4] } 8... Nbd7 { Black's best continuation. } { [%csl Bd7] } (8... b5 { Most common continuation. } 9. O-O Bb7 10. a4! $14 { We
switch to positional chess. } { [%csl Ba4][%cal Rb5b4,Gc3d5] }) 9. O-O { We continue developing. } 9... Be7 { The best. } 10. g4! $146 { We must play concretely to achieve
anything. } { [%csl Bg4] } 10... Nc5 { Human move. } (10... Qc6! { Critical move. } { [%csl Bc6,Re4] } 11. Bf3 { Defending e4. } 11... h6 { [%csl Yc6,Yf3][%cal Rc3d5] } 12. Kh1!? $132 { I recommend this quiet move now. It's definitely useful, and asks Black what they want to do now. After all, Black still
needs to develop. Later seen in bigger detail in Chapter 5. } { [%csl Bh1] }) 11. Bf3 { We simply defend e4. } 11... Nxb3 12. axb3 exf4 13. Bxf4 $14 { White has a
safe advantage. }) (7... Nc6 { This is a very serious move that Jose recommended in the course "Najdorf for Black". You should definitely
check it out in Chapter 6, since it is a concrete line. }) 8. g4 { We utilize Black's previous move, and we can clearly see
why we chose Nb3. } { [%csl Yb3,Yd7,Gg4] } 8... d5! { The most critical move. } { [%csl Bd5] } (8... h6 { The most common reply. It's a logical measure against
g5, but } { [%cal Rg4g5] } 9. g5 { We can play it anyway. } { [%csl Bg5] } 9... Ng8! { The only good move. } { [%csl Bg8] } (9... hxg5? { The most common by far (19/20
games), but this is a serious mistake! } 10. fxg5 Nh5 { Looks fine for Black at first, but after } 11. Qf3! { Black is actually in
huge trouble already. } { [%csl Bf3] } 11... Be7 { Most common. } 12. h4! { Important move, we must protect g5. } { [%csl Bh4] } 12... Nb6 { Best idea for
Black. } { [%cal Gc8e6,Gb6c4] } 13. Be3 Be6 14. O-O-O Nc4 15. Bxc4 { We trade Black's best pieces now. } 15... Bxc4 16. Nd5 Bxd5 17. exd5 Qd7 18. Nd2 $16 { The knight has a nice outpost on e4, and White has a very pleasant advantage. } { [%csl Be4,Rh5][%cal Gd2e4,Gh1f1] }) 10. Qh5!? $146 { My recommendation. } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Gg5g6] } 10... g6 { Human move. } 11. Qh4 Bg7 { Most logical. } { [%cal Gh6g5] } 12. Qg3 Ne7 13. h4 { Trying to play h5. } { [%cal Gh4h5] } 13... h5 { [%cal Rh4h5] } 14. Bh3 exf4 { [%cal Gd7e5] } 15. Bxd7+! { Important trade to limit Black's counterplay. } 15... Bxd7 16. Bxf4 Bg4 { Against 000. } { [%cal Re1c1] } 17. Bxd6 O-O 18. O-O $14 { White is a pawn up and has nice play. }) 9. exd5 Nb6 { [%csl Rd5] } 10. g5 { The solid option, forcing
the endgame. } 10... Nfxd5 11. Bg2 Nxf4 { Critical. } 12. Qxd8+ Kxd8 13. Bxf4 exf4 14. Rf1!? $146 { Interesting
move order to exclude Bg4 ideas. } { [%csl Bf1][%cal Rc8g4] } 14... Bd6 { Critical, of course, as Black keeps the extra pawn. } 15. O-O-O Kc7 16. a4 { The
critical position, where our main goal is a5-Nd5. and it is analyzed in bigger detail in Chapter 3. } { [%cal Ga4a5,Gc3d5] } *


