1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f4 e5 { In this chapter, we start looking at the most common and principled approach. } 7. Nb3 { My recommendation. This is the only interesting approach for White. In this chapter, we will start looking at Black's
rare options. } { [%csl Bb3] } (7. Nf3 { The old mainline, but it's not interesting nowadays. } 7... Nbd7 { Black simply defends e5 and wants
to get typical counterplay with b5. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gb7b5] } 8. a4 { White shouldn't allow b5. } { [%cal Rb7b5] } (8. Bc4 { White also tried activating the bishop first. } 8... b5! { But now this comes with tempo. } { [%csl Bb5] } 9. Bd5 Rb8 { Black has nothing to worry about; it's even slightly
preferable already for Black. } { [%cal Gf8e7,Ge8g8] } (9... Nxd5 10. Nxd5 $132 { Also, this is fine for Black. }) 10. Ng5 { Simply met with } { [%csl Rf7] } 10... Nxd5 11. Qxd5 Qe7 $15 { White has no attack, and Black has a better game with the pair of bishops. } { [%csl Bc8,Bf8][%cal Gh7h6,Gd7f6] }) 8... Qc7! { Most accurate move order for Black. Without Bc4, this line is very harmless for Black. For example } { [%csl Bc7][%cal Rf1c4] } (8... Be7 { Most common,
but it allows } 9. Bc4 $132 { Still, Black should be fine, but it's more or less playable now for White. } { [%csl Gc4] }) 9. Bd3 Be7 10. O-O O-O 11. Kh1 { This is how most games continue. } 11... Nc5 12. Qe1 Re8 { Very simple play for Black and I don't see any problems White can pose. } 13. Be3 (13. fxe5 { Most common, the idea is } 13... dxe5 14. Qg3 { To put some pressure on e5, but } { [%csl Re5] } 14... Be6! { Black can simply give it
up. } { [%csl Be6] } 15. Qxe5 (15. Ng5 { Most played, but after } 15... Nxd3 16. Nxe6 fxe6 17. cxd3 { The doubled e-pawns are actually quite useful,
as they control many key squares. } { [%csl Be5,Be6] } 17... Rf8 $15 { Black is for choice here. }) 15... Bd6 16. Qg5 h6 { Black gets many tempi for the pawn. } 17. Qh4 Ng4 18. Bd2 Rad8 $36 { This is very tough to play for White, despite the extra pawn. Black has huge compensation, and scored 3.5/4 in
practice. }) 13... Bd7 (13... exf4 { Also fine. } 14. Bxf4 Bd7 { Black has no problems. }) 14. fxe5 dxe5 15. Qg3 { Now Black is
fine with many moves, Rad8 for example, or } { [%cal Ga8d8] } 15... Bd8 { Played in Gabrielian-Giri, 2025. Giri simply defended e5 and had a great Najdorf and
won later. }) 7... exf4 { Seems a bit strange, so it has not been tried often, only eleven games here. } (7... Be6? { Typical in
many Najdorf, but here it's just bad. } { [%csl Re6] } 8. f5! { Of course, we use the chance to push the pawn forward with tempo. } { [%csl Bf5] } 8... Bd7 (8... Bxb3 { Black doesn't want to waste a tempo, but this is strange. } 9. axb3 Be7 10. Bg5 Nbd7 { Was played in Goryachkina-Radnaev, 2025. White
continued with Bc4, but we can improve with } 11. Qf3 $146 { It's definitely stronger to go for long castling here. } { [%cal Ge1c1] } 11... b5 12. O-O-O h6 13. Bxf6 { Engine's best. } (13. Bh4 $16 { Also fine for an advantage. }) 13... Nxf6 14. Kb1 $16 { White has a
very comfortable advantage due to control over d5 and good potential on the kingside. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Gg2g4,Gh2h4] }) (8... Bc8 9. g4 $16 { Similar to 8...Bd7. }) 9. g4 { Clearly, f5 is useful as we see, so I don't see why Black would provoke it. } { [%csl Bf5,Rf6][%cal Gg4g5] } 9... b5 10. a3 { No need to
allow b4. } { [%cal Rb5b4] } 10... h6 { Black also should stop g5. } { [%cal Rg4g5] } 11. h4 Nc6 12. Be3 Rb8 13. Bd3 $16 { Qf3 next, White has a nice spatial
advantage and is just better. } { [%cal Gd1f3] }) (7... b5?! { Premature, due to } 8. fxe5 dxe5 9. Qxd8+ Kxd8 10. Bg5 $16 { And we can
play against b5 quickly with a4. } { [%csl Rb5][%cal Ga2a4] }) (7... Bg4 { Was tried in an old game in 1994. } 8. Be2 Bd7 { Engine's recommendation. } (8... Bxe2?! { Not a
good trade. } 9. Qxe2 $16 { Clearly, this trade only helped White. } { [%csl Rc8][%cal Gc1e3,Ge1c1,Bg2g4] }) 9. Be3 { But
it seems weird to play like this, as Be2 is clearly a more useful inclusion than Bd7. For example } { [%csl Rd7,Be2][%cal Gg2g4] } 9... Nc6 10. Qd2 Be7 11. O-O-O O-O { Logical
development. } 12. Kb1 { Typical prophylaxis. } 12... b5 13. Rhg1 $14 { With a good version for White of a typical Najdorf. } { [%cal Gf4f5,Gg2g4] }) 8. Bxf4 Be7 { The best try. } (8... Nc6?! { Most common, but dubious, as Black's priority should be to castle short. } 9. Qe2 { Planning long castling. } { [%cal Ge1c1] } 9... Be6 (9... Be7 { Black switches to short castling, but wasted a move with Nc6 now. } 10. h3! { Important move, to enable long castling and prepare some future g4. For example } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Ge1c1,Rc8g4,Gg2g4] } 10... b5 11. O-O-O Qc7 12. g4 { g5 is already deadly, so Black must play } { [%cal Gg4g5,Gc3d5] } 12... b4 13. Nd5 Nxd5 14. exd5 { But this looks extremely shaky for Black now. } 14... Ne5 15. Qd2 { Creating the threat Bxe5-d6. } { [%cal Gf4e5,Gd5d6] } 15... Ng6 16. Bg3 { Now, b4 is also hanging. } { [%csl Rb4] } 16... a5 17. Nd4 $16 { White is much better and can continue to improve easily. } { [%cal Bd4c6,Gd4f5,Gh3h4,Gh4h5] }) (9... Bg4 { Black attempts to prevent long castling. } 10. Qd2 Nh5 { Engine's try, but this is very artificial. } 11. Be3 Ne5 12. Be2 { This trade is good for us; we are also able to play
long castling again. } { [%cal Ge1c1] } 12... Bxe2 13. Qxe2 Qh4+ 14. Bf2 Qg5 { Black can successfully stop long castling here, but it's far
from equal. We can simply play } { [%cal Gf2e3,Gg5h4,Re1c1] } 15. Nd5 Rc8 { Must be played. } { [%cal Rd5c7] } 16. O-O Nf4 { Looks very risky, but it's the best. } 17. Nxf4 Qxf4 18. h3 { Just some prophylaxis. No need to rush, White is comfortable with the lead in development. } { [%cal Re5g4,Rf4g4] } 18... Be7 19. Nd4 g6 { [%cal Rd4f5] } 20. Rad1 $16 { And White is positionally better. }) 10. h3! { Important move. And via transposition, we reached a position
with seventy-three games, but Black has a pretty bad score, as the position is just much better for White. For example } { [%csl Bh3] } 10... g6 { The best attempt. Black aims to play Nh5, and g4 is met with h5. But we can simply play } { [%cal Gf6h5,Yg2g4,Yh7h5] } (10... Ne5 { Second most common
move. } 11. O-O-O Qc7 12. Nd4 { Centralizing the knight again. } 12... g6 $146 { In two games, Black didn't play this move, but then just
Nf5 is crushing, so this is really necessary. } { [%cal Rd4f5] } 13. Qd2 Be7 14. Be2 $16 { White has a comfortable advantage. We are fully
developed and can continue with g4-g5 next. } { [%cal Gg2g4,Gg4g5] }) (10... Be7 { Most common, but this is very passive. } 11. O-O-O Qc7 12. g4 O-O { How most games
continued, and also Nepo played it for Black, but it's objectively close to being lost already! } 13. g5 Nd7 { Van Foreest played Qe3 here and
won against Nepo here, but even stronger was } 14. Nd5 Bxd5 15. exd5 Nce5 16. h4 $18 { And Black simply has no
counterplay, so White has a crushing advantage. } { [%cal Gh4h5,Gc1b1,Gb3d4] }) 11. Qe3 Nh5 12. Bg5 { Not allowing Qh4+. } { [%cal Rd8h4] } 12... Be7 13. Bxe7 Qxe7 14. O-O-O { Black
managed to trade bishops, but it's not helping much. } 14... Nf6 15. Be2 O-O 16. Rhf1 $16 { White is just better,
and g6 was also weakening. } { [%csl Rg6][%cal Gb3d4,Gh3h4,Gh4h5] }) 9. h3!? { The simplest and most practical approach. We take control of g4 and keep our queen
flexible. } { [%csl Bh3] } 9... b5 $146 { Most challenging, but it's a novelty. } (9... Nc6 10. Qe2 { Transposes into 8...Nc6. }) (9... Be6 { Typical move, and here
we can develop with } 10. Qf3 { More active than Qe2. } 10... Nc6 11. O-O-O { Black has no tempo with Ne5 here, as we already
castled. } { [%csl Bd1][%cal Rc6e5] } 11... Qc7 12. g4 { Preparing g5, so Black will probably play } { [%cal Gg4g5] } 12... h6 { [%cal Rg4g5] } 13. Kb1 Ne5 14. Qg2 $16 { The queen is very well placed here, as it defends h1 so h4-g5 can come quickly. } { [%csl Bg2][%cal Bh3h4,Bg4g5] }) (9... O-O { Logical development. } 10. Qe2! { It's important not to allow Nh5 here. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Rf6h5] } (10. Qd3?! { Doesn't transpose into 9...b5, as Black will play } 10... Nh5 $132 { With great counterplay, suddenly. } { [%csl Rf4,Bh5][%cal Ge7h4] }) 10... Be6 11. g4 h6 { Black, of course, doesn't want to allow
g5. } { [%cal Rg4g5] } 12. O-O-O Nbd7 13. Rg1 { White has a simple plan, but here Black can organize some counterplay with } { [%cal Gh3h4,Gg4g5] } 13... Rc8 { Followed by Rxc3, so this is important to know: } { [%cal Gc8c3] } 14. g5! { Only move for the advantage. } { [%csl Bg5] } 14... hxg5 15. Rxg5 { Black has no good discovered attack on g5 and Qg2 is coming, but } { [%csl Rg7][%cal Ge2g2] } 15... Rxc3! { Of course, Black seeks counterplay. } 16. bxc3 Qb6 17. Qg2 Ne8 { Only move. } (17... g6? { Very bad due to } 18. Nd4 $18 { And Black is busted. } { [%csl Re6][%cal Gg5g6,Gd4e6] }) 18. h4! $16 { The most precise, and now White has a nice positional advantage. } { [%csl Bh4][%cal Gf1e2,Gc1b2,Be7g5,Bh4g5] }) 10. Qd3 { My
