1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f4 e5 7. Nb3 Be7 { This is Black's second most common move and a very logical one. } 8. fxe5! { This is the key way. } 8... dxe5 9. Qxd8+ Bxd8 { Until a few years ago, nobody would believe that this is a serious approach for White,
but modern engines have changed this common opinion, and playing from Black's side is far from simple. } 10. Be3 { The most logical
move. } { [%csl Be3] } 10... Nbd7 { The most common move. } (10... Be6 { was recently played by GM L'Ami. } 11. Nc5! { A very important idea. If Black
just manages to develop Be6+Nbd7 without any problem, then his position is absolutely fine. } { [%csl Bc5,Re6] } (11. h3?! { Black continues with } 11... Nbd7 $15 { and the endgame suddenly becomes more comfortable for Black! }) 11... b5 (11... b6 12. Nxe6 fxe6 13. O-O-O Ke7 { Maurizzi-Lamard, 2023. Now White had to continue with the logical } 14. a4 $16 { and White has a
stable advantage thanks to the two bishops, the better structure and the three vs two on the queenside. } { [%csl Ba4][%cal Rb6b5,Gf1e2] }) (11... Ba5 { is not a problem as we might just continue with the natural } { [%csl Rc3,Re4][%cal Ga5e1,Gf6e4] } 12. Bd3 $14) 12. Nxe6 $146 (12. Bd3?! { wrong approach from
Sindarov. } 12... Nbd7! 13. Nxe6 fxe6 { and now Black is doing very fine as after } 14. a4 b4 15. Na2 a5 16. Nc1 { our bishop
on d3 is misplaced as it stops Nd3, and also Black has the chance to continue with } { [%csl Rd3][%cal Gc1d3] } 16... Bb6 { with full equality. }) 12... fxe6 13. a4! { asking immediate questions. In general, you should play this endgame actively with White. } { [%csl Ba4] } 13... b4 14. Na2 { [%csl Rb4] } 14... a5 15. Bb5+ Nbd7 16. Nc1 $16 { Nd3-O-O-O next. White has a stable advantage. } { [%cal Gc1d3,Ge1c1] }) (10... b5?! { premature. } 11. a4! $146 { As always, we must play active chess. } { [%csl Ba4,Rb5][%cal Ga4b5] } 11... bxa4 (11... b4 12. Nd5 Nxe4 (12... Nxd5 13. exd5 $16 { O-O-O next. } { [%cal Ge1c1] }) 13. Nxb4 $16) 12. Rxa4 $16 { stable advantage. } { [%csl Ba4,Ra6][%cal Gf1d3,Ge1e2] }) (10... Nc6 { Common move, but the knight doesn't
really belong on c6 in this structure. } { [%csl Bc6] } 11. O-O-O Bg4 { most common move, and the best according to the engine. } (11... O-O 12. Be2 $14 { Bf3-Nd5 next. } { [%cal Ge2f3,Gc3d5] }) 12. Rd3 $146 { The best square, as we defend our Be3. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Gd3e3] } (12. Rd2 { it's more difficult to get something, as after } { [%csl Rd2,Re3] } 12... O-O 13. Nc5 { Black has } 13... Bb6! $13 { and here
we see the difference between 12.Rd2 and 12.Rd3. } { [%csl Bb6,Re3][%cal Gb6e3] }) 12... O-O (12... Be7 13. Nc5 b5 14. h3 Bc8 15. Nd5 $16) 13. Nc5 { Attacking
b7. } { [%csl Rb7] } 13... b5 14. h3 { Black's bishop doesn't really have a lot of available squares. } { [%csl Rg4] } 14... Bc8 15. Nd5 $16 { We see once again
that one of the main drawbacks for Black of having the knight on c6 is that Nd5 becomes much more difficult to handle for him. } { [%csl Rc6,Bd5] }) (10... O-O { I don't really like this move because in the future, when Black will play Nbd7-b6-Bb7, there will be no king on e8
to protect Nd7. } { [%csl Rg8][%cal Rb8d7] } 11. h3 { My recommendation. Preventing Ng4 or Bg4 ideas, and preparing a possible
kingside expansion with g2-g4. } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Rf6g4,Gg2g4,Rc8g4] } 11... b5 { [%cal Gf6e4] } (11... h5?! 12. Be2 $14 { h3-h5 was a good inclusion for White,
as the h5-pawn might often be hanging in some lines. } { [%csl Be2,Rh5][%cal Ge2h5] }) 12. O-O-O (12. a4? { not good here because after } 12... b4 13. Nd5 Nxe4! $17 { Black has Ng3 or Bh4+ idea, and we cannot stop both of them. } { [%cal Ge4g3,Gd8h4] }) 12... Bb7 (12... b4?! { but now this is too risky: } 13. Nd5 Nxe4?! { Black has the Ng3 idea, but not the Bh4+ one, so after } { [%cal Re4g3] } 14. Bd3 Ng3 15. Rhe1 $16 { We have not only
compensation, but also a good advantage. } { [%cal Gd5b6,Gd5b4] }) 13. g4 { preparing Bg2. } { [%cal Gf1g2] } 13... h6 { Black's best according to the engine, and here we can
