also good enough for some advantage. } { [%csl Ba5] }) 14... Rb8 15. Nd3! { [%csl Re5] } 15... Nfd7 16. g4 h6 17. h4 f6 { Shevchenko-Kempinski, 2022. Here White's
most direct approach was enough for a big advantage: } 18. g5! hxg5 19. hxg5 Rxh1 20. Rxh1 fxg5 21. Rg1 Kf6 22. Rf1+ Ke7 23. Rf5 $16) (11... b5?! { once again premature. } 12. a4! b4 13. Nd5 Bb7 (13... Nxe4 14. Nxb4 $16 { [%cal Ge1c1] }) (13... Rb8 { doesn't help because
after } 14. O-O-O! { it's still very dangerous for Black to capture on e4. } 14... Nxe4 15. Rhe1 O-O 16. Bc4 $16 { Huge difference in piece
activity. }) 14. O-O-O Bxd5 { played by the strong GM Saric, but it led to a total disaster. } (14... Nxd5 15. exd5 Rc8 16. Rhf1 O-O 17. Bg4 $18) 15. exd5 Bb6 16. Bxb6 Nxb6 17. Nc5 O-O 18. d6 $18 { Radovanovic-Saric, 2023. }) 12. O-O-O Bb7 13. Rhf1 $14 { For the engine this is only
+0.40, but over the board it feels much more. For example, in a recent game Black played } 13... Rc8?! { which was already a mistake, although it's
