1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f4 e5 7. Nb3 Qc7 { Very serious try, which MVL, Nepo, Sarana, and many strong players
have used. } 8. Be2! { The best chance. We are ready for both castling kingside and sometimes g4 ideas. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Ge1g1,Gg2g4] } 8... Nbd7 { Black's best continuation. It's a logical development move, and compared to other options we saw, this one has no clear
drawback. } { [%csl Bd7] } (8... Be6 { There are 620 games here, but mostly transpositions from the 6.Be2 variation. But this position is quite shaky for
Black. } 9. g4 { We carry on with the kingside advance, and it already looks critical for Black. } { [%cal Gf4f5,Gg4g5] } 9... b5 { Engine's best, but
after } (9... h6 { Logical against g5, but } { [%cal Rg4g5] } 10. g5! { It works well anyway. } { [%csl Bg5] } 10... hxg5 11. fxg5 Nfd7 12. h4 Nc6 13. Be3 $16 { Qd2-0-0-0 next, and White is just better. } { [%cal Gd1d2,Ge1c1] }) (9... exf4 { Most common. } 10. Bxf4 { But this helps with our
development. } 10... h6 { Black really must prevent g5 here. } { [%cal Rg4g5] } (10... Nc6?! { Most played, but it's bad as it allows } 11. g5 Nd7 12. Qd2 Be7 13. O-O-O Nce5 { How most games went, but it's very bad for Black. } 14. Nd4 g6 15. h4 O-O-O 16. Nd5 { The simplest. } (16. Rh3!? $18 { Flashy move and just as good. } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Re6h3,Bc3d5] }) 16... Bxd5 17. exd5 $18 { Rh3-c3 is coming, and Black is
just lost. } { [%cal Gh1h3,Gh3c3] }) 11. Qd2 Nc6 12. O-O-O O-O-O 13. h4 $16 { With a pleasant advantage for White. A possible plan is Rh3
with the idea Nd5-Rc3. } { [%cal Bh3c3,Bh1h3,Bc3d5] }) 10. g5 b4 11. f5! { We get exactly what we wanted. } { [%csl Re6,Bf5,Rf6,Bg5] } 11... Bd7 $146 { Necessary. } (11... Bc4?! { Black went wrong in all the games here. } 12. Bxc4 Qxc4 13. Na5! { Nice attack on the queen. The point is } { [%csl Ba5] } 13... Qc5 14. gxf6 bxc3 15. b4! $18 { And the only way for Black to stop Qd5 is Qd4, but the endgame is also winning for White. } { [%csl Bb4][%cal Gd1d5] }) 12. Nd5 { Not
the only, but the simplest path to an advantage. } 12... Nxd5 13. Qxd5 Bc6 14. Qc4 $16 { White is just better. }) (8... b5 { Most common
continuation. } 9. O-O Bb7 10. a4! { But we can switch to a positional approach now. } { [%csl Ba4] } 10... b4 11. Nd5 Nxd5 (11... Bxd5?! { Doesn't
make too much sense. } 12. exd5 Nbd7 13. g4 $16 { The f6-knight is a target and Black clearly misses the dark-squared
