equalize. }) 15. bxc3 Be7 16. Bc4 $36 { With a good version of the mainline. Nothing is forced so we get a game. } { [%cal Gb3d2,Gd2f3] }) 14. Qd3!? { Interesting, trying to avoid Be6. It can be a surprise, and it was not analyzed in our course for Black! } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Rc8e6] } (14. Qf3 { What we analyzed in our course for Black, and it was used by Pranesh. You can try this line to check your opponent. } 14... Be6!! { Very accurate move by Lu Shanglei. } { [%csl Be6] } 15. Ba4 (15. fxe6? { Mistake. } 15... Qxf3 16. gxf3 axb5 $17 { Black is better in the endgame. } { [%csl Rc3] }) 15... b5 { Forced line: } 16. Nxd4 exd4 17. O-O Bd6!! { Key move. } { [%csl Bd6] } 18. fxe6 Qxf3 19. Nd5+ Kxe6 20. gxf3 bxa4 { After the
simplifications, the endgame is balanced. } 21. Rxa4 Rhb8 22. b3 f5 { With some key moves, Black solved all problems. }) 14... Rd8! { Most accurate reply. } { [%csl Bd8] } (14... dxc3 { Very logical to start with, but here we get a game. } 15. bxc3 Rd8 16. Qe3 Bd6 17. Bc4 Bc7 { Very logical play by both sides. } 18. Bd5 { Against b5. } { [%cal Rb7b5] } 18... Bb6 19. Qg3 Bd7 { Engine's best, giving up a pawn to
finish development. } { [%cal Gd7c6] } (19... Kf8 { Also very logical. } 20. c4 { We take space. } { [%cal Gc4c5] } 20... Qe7 21. Rf1 a5! { Only move to hold the balance. } 22. c5 Bc7 { Best. } (22... Bxc5?! { Small mistake due to } 23. f6 gxf6 24. Nxc5 Qxc5 25. Rxf6 $14 { And Black has many
weaknesses, so Rxd5 is forced, but the compensation is not easy to prove and probably not full. } { [%csl Re5,Rf7] }) 23. Qe3 $132 { And here it's objectively
balanced, but I like White's chances. We have more space, and it seems riskier for Black. }) 20. Bxb7 Ra7 21. Bd5 $36 { We take the pawn, and Black must prove the compensation here. Our plan is simple: we will improve the knight to c4 or go
with the pawn to c4 if necessary. } { [%cal Gb3d2,Gd2c4,Bc3c4] }) 15. O-O-O axb5 (15... Bxc3 { Leads to the same. } 16. bxc3 axb5 17. Qxb5 { Transposes into 15...axb5. }) 16. Qxb5 Bxc3 17. bxc3 { Of course, the position is close to full equality, but I think White has no risk and can get some practical chances. } 17... Kf8! { Best for the engine, but this is very cold. The point is to bring the king into safety and even give the d4 pawn
sometimes. } { [%cal Gf8g8] } (17... Qd6 { Very logical way to look for counterplay. } { [%cal Gd6a3] } 18. cxd4 exd4 { Black relies on Ra1+ in
case of Rxd4, but after } { [%cal Rd1d4,Ga8a1] } 19. Rd3 { It looks like Black could get in some trouble soon, as we plan Rhd1, and then the
pawn is hanging. For example } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Gh1d1] } 19... Bd7 20. Qxb7 { We can take the pawn, as Kd2 is always a suitable reply to Qa3+. } { [%cal Yd6a3,Yc1d2] } 20... Rdb8 21. Qd5 { It's equal, but Black has to be careful now what to do. After all we are a pawn up and d4 is about to fall as well. } { [%csl Rd4] } 21... Qa3+ (21... Qxd5 22. exd5 Bxf5 23. Rxd4 $44 { No Rb3 tactics due to Re1+ intermezzo. Black has compensation, but needs to prove
it, while White has no risk here. } { [%cal Rb8b3,Gh1e1] }) 22. Kd1 Ba4 23. Ke2 { We just bring the king to safety. } 23... Bb5 (23... Bxb3 24. cxb3 { b3
most likely falls, but so does d4, and in the end, the endgame should be a draw, but only White keeps small chances with the extra pawn. } { [%csl Rb3,Rd4] }) 24. Rc1! { Important move, we are not worried about } { [%csl Bc1] } 24... Bxd3+ 25. cxd3 { As Black must deal with Rc7+. } { [%cal Gc1c7] } 25... Qd6 { Most
logical. } 26. Qxd6+ Kxd6 27. Nxd4 { And it's an equal endgame, but only White can potentially be slightly better and press. }) 18. cxd4 exd4 19. Rhe1 (19. Kb1 { Another possible try. } 19... Bd7 20. Qc5+ Kg8 21. Rxd4 Bc6 22. g3 $44 { And maybe we can play
with Rhd1 instead of e1. It's equal for the engine anyway, but maybe there is a practical difference. } { [%csl Bh1][%cal Bh1d1,Gh1e1] }) 19... Bd7 { Now the engine brings the
bishop. } 20. Qb4+ Kg8 21. Rxd4 { White takes a pawn, but after } (21. Qxd4 { Gives Black the chance for } 21... Qg5+ 22. Qe3 Qf6 { And we have nothing better than to repeat. } { [%cal Ge3d4,Gf6g5] }) 21... Bc6 { Black has full compensation and will equalize. For example } 22. g3 { We
can try to keep the pawns alive somehow, but Black has many moves. I don't think White risks anything here, but winning this will also be
tough. For example } 22... Ra4 23. Rxd8+ Qxd8 24. Qd2 { Of course, Black will not trade queens but play } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Rd8d2,Gb3d2] } 24... Qe7 { [%csl Re4][%cal Ge7a3] } 25. e5 Qa3+ { And we cannot avoid the repetition. } { [%cal Rc1d1,Gc6f3] } 26. Kb1 Qa2+ 27. Kc1 { Draw in the end. 7...Nc6
followed by 8...d5 is a very serious try, and there is no way to refute it; that's why we also recommended it for Black. We can try to play
concretely and check our opponent, but if they are super well prepared and play like an engine, the game ends logically in a draw. But there
are many tries you can choose, and I doubt many players will be so well prepared here, so we should get chances in a practical game. } *


