1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f4 e6 { Second most common and very playable line. } 7. Qf3!? { My recommendation, we simply want to develop the queenside, and keep the g4 resource in reserve. } { [%cal Gc1e3,Ge1c1,Bg2g4] } (7. g4?! { a bit
premature } 7... e5! 8. Nf5 Nc6 $132 { g6 to follow, and the setup is quite vulnerable } { [%csl Rf5,Rg4][%cal Gg7g6,Gh7h5] } 9. g5 Nxe4! $17 { The point. } { [%csl Be4,Rf5] }) 7... Qb6 { Main line, tried often by legendary GM Kasparov. } (7... Be7?! { Slow development poses no problems. } 8. Be3 O-O 9. O-O-O $18 { White's ready to break in the center already. The score is close to 80% for White. } { [%cal Ge4e5,Gg2g4] }) (7... Nbd7?! { Same slow nature. } 8. g4 h6 9. Be2!? { Accurate move, to give g4-pawn some protection. } { [%csl Gg4] } 9... Qb6 { Short-Kasparov, 1989. } 10. Nb3 Qc7 11. a4! $146 { Improvement, to stop b5. } { [%cal Rb7b5] } 11... b6 12. g5 { Let's open the position: } 12... hxg5 13. fxg5 Ne5 14. Qg3 Nfd7 15. Bf4 $16 { Everything developed perfectly, and Black has in fact zero counterplay. } { [%cal Ge1c1,Gh2h4] }) (7... Qc7?! { Popular reply, with 750 games,
but quite dubious. } 8. g4! { The inclusion is definitely in White's favor. } { [%csl Yc7,Gf3] } 8... Nc6 { Only logical move. } (8... e5 { to compare: } 9. Nf5 Nc6 (9... g6? { even worse } 10. fxe5 dxe5 11. Bg5 $18 { Too many issues. } { [%csl Rc7,Rf6][%cal Gc3d5,Gf5e3] }) 10. g5! { There is no more Nxe4 trick. } { [%cal Rf6e4,Gf3e4] } 10... Nd4 11. Nxd4 exd4 12. Ne2 $16 { d4 will fall and White is much better. } { [%csl Rd4] }) 9. Nxc6! { We must play concretely. } { [%csl Gc6] } 9... bxc6 { to at least have the d5 break } { [%cal Gd6d5] } (9... Qxc6 { doesn't make sense } 10. g5 Nd7 11. h4 b5 12. a3 $16 { White has a huge spatial advantage. } { [%csl Gf4,Gg5,Gh4][%cal Gc1e3,Ge1c1,Gh4h5] }) 10. g5 Nd7 11. Bd2!? { I
like this semi-prophylactic move, giving the c3-knight some support. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gd2c3] } (11. Be3 { to highlight: } 11... d5 12. O-O-O Bb4 $13 { Some issues
with the knight. } { [%csl Rc3] }) 11... d5 12. h4 { Bb4 makes no sense now. } { [%cal Rf8b4] } 12... d4!? $146 { Engine's recommendation. } (12... Rb8 { happened in one
email game } 13. O-O-O d4 14. Na4 $16 { Bc4-b3 next and White has a huge advantage. } { [%cal Gf1c4,Gb2b3] }) 13. Na4 c5 14. b3! $16 { Typical defensive setup, and White has a safe positional advantage. } { [%csl Gb3][%cal Ga4b2,Gh4h5] }) (7... e5!? { As weird as it looks,
this is the engine's reply. But very rare move, only twenty games. } { [%csl Be5] } 8. fxe5 dxe5 9. Nf5 { Another nice fact of Qf3. } { [%cal Gf3f5] } 9... Bxf5!? { Black takes sooner or later. } 10. Qxf5 Qc7 { Strubbs-Plume, email 2022. } (10... Nc6 11. Bd3 Bb4 12. O-O $14 { I
understand it's playable, but White has a pair of bishops and f-file advantage for free. } { [%csl Gc1,Gd3][%cal Gf1f7] }) 11. a3!? { Logical, to stop Bb4. } { [%cal Rf8b4] } 11... Nc6 12. Bd3 Bc5 { Black logically stops castle, but it's not an issue. } { [%cal Rc5g1] } 13. Rf1! { White can still use
the f-file. } { [%csl Rf6][%cal Gf1f7] } 13... Nd4!? $146 { Best. } (13... O-O?! { played in the game, but quite unfortunate } 14. Bg5 Ne7 15. Qh3 { Big pressure on
f6. } { [%csl Rf6] } 15... Bd4 16. Ne2! $146 { A bit scary, but the best. } { [%csl Be2] } 16... Bxb2 17. Rb1 Qa5+ 18. Bd2 Qxa3 { and here comes the key idea: } 19. Rxf6!! { Exchange sacrifice to start an attack. } { [%csl Bf6,Rg8] } 19... Qa2! { Best defense. } (19... gxf6 20. Qh6 Ng6 21. Ng3 $16 { White will as minimum regain the exchange and keep a big advantage. } { [%csl Rg8][%cal Gg3h5,Gh5f6] }) 20. Rxb2 Qxb2 21. Rf3 $16 { The minor
pieces should be faster at organizing the attack I feel. No human will Black will defend this. } { [%csl Gd2,Gd3,Ge2] }) 14. Qh3 Qb6 15. Nd5!? { to transform the
structure } 15... Nxd5 16. exd5 { Now Black's king is also stuck in the center. } { [%csl Re8][%cal Re8g8,Re8c8] } 16... Qd8 { Most logical. } { [%csl Rd5] } 17. Be4 Nb5 18. Qg3 $14 { The game remains complicated, but in White's favor a bit. (...Qe7! only move for example). This e6-e5 line is very
unpractical for Black, and unlikely to happen. }) 8. a3!? { Difficult to say which one is better,
but compared to 9.Nb3 there are fewer games, and it seems that engines prefer it slightly more. Overall this move is quite useful,
preventing any b5-b4. And also Nd4 can't be taken due to Be3. } { [%csl Ba3][%cal Yb7b5,Yb5b4,Rb6d4,Gc1e3] } 8... Nc6 { Most common by far. } (8... Qxd4?? 9. Be3 $18 { The point, the
queen is trapped. } { [%csl Rd4] }) (8... Nbd7?! { tried by Kasparov a few times, but after: } 9. Be2! $146 { We reach something that resembles
7...Nbd7. } { [%csl Be2] } 9... d5 { Engine's best, but very artificial. } (9... Be7 10. Bd2 $16 { Long castle next, with a large advantage. } { [%cal Ge1c1] }) 10. exd5 Nxd5 11. Nxd5 exd5 12. Qe3+! { Accurate check. } { [%csl Ge3] } 12... Kd8 { Best try. } (12... Be7 13. Nf5 Qxe3 14. Bxe3 $18 { The
endgame is objectively winning for White. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Ge3d4] }) 13. Bd2 { Simple development. } 13... Bc5 14. Bc3 Nf6 15. O-O-O Re8 16. Qd3 $16 { Black isn't totally lost after Ke7, but still, White keeps a large advantage. } { [%cal Bd8e7,Gd4b3] }) (8... Be7 { approved by the engine, but seems slow } 9. Bd3 { We still ask Black to reveal the cards. } 9... h5! $146 { Engine suggest to play prophylactically, but of course, it's quite deep. } { [%cal Rg2g4] } (9... Nc6 10. Nxc6 bxc6 11. e5! $16 { leads to similar play to the main line }) (9... e5 10. Nb3 exf4 11. Bxf4 $14 { Dream scenario for
White, full development and weak d5-square to play for. }) 10. Bd2!? { Let's finish development. } { [%csl Bd2][%cal Ge1c1] } 10... Nc6 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. e5 dxe5 { This version is a bit improved, thanks to Ng4 resource, but we can play the tricky: } { [%cal Rf4e5,Gf6g4] } 13. Na4! { Intermezzo. } { [%csl Rb6] } 13... Qc7 14. O-O-O! { Pawn sacrifice. } 14... Rb8 15. Rhe1 $36 { Probably, this is the best Black can
achieve in the whole 7...Qb6 line, but still, White's initiative is very big, +0,30-40 for the engine. } { [%csl Gc3,Re5][%cal Gd2c3,Gc3e5] }) 9. Nxc6! { We take of course. } 9... bxc6 { Necessary. } (9... Qxc6?! { as usual bad } 10. Be3 { leads to similar position seen before, let's see how things can
develop: } { [%cal Ge1c1,Gg2g4] } 10... Be7 11. O-O-O b5 12. g4 Bb7 13. Bd3 O-O? { played by my opponent, but runs directly into the attack } 14. Qh3!? { Very
typical idea. } { [%csl Bh3] } 14... d5 15. exd5 exd5 16. Bd4 $18 { Total domination, and Black soon collapsed. Ceres-Scurtu,
1-0, Chania 2023. } { [%csl Gd3,Gd4,Rg8,Gh3][%cal Gh1e1] }) 10. e5! { That's another reason why I like 8.a3, in the main line we reach our typical play against Black's
structure. } { [%csl Ge5] } 10... Nd7 { Best. } (10... dxe5?! { less appealing } 11. fxe5 Nd7 12. Qg3 $16 { issues with development } { [%csl Rg7] }) 11. exd6 Bxd6 12. Qg3! $146 { Annoying tempo. } { [%csl Rg7] } 12... Be7! { Let's check the engine line: } (12... O-O 13. Ne4 Be7 14. Bd2 $16 { White's attack is quite dangerous. } { [%csl Rg8][%cal Gd2c3,Gf1d3] }) 13. Qxg7 Bh4+! { The point, to weaken some squares. } (13... Bf6 14. Qg3 $16 { Just pawn up. }) 14. g3 Bf6 15. Qh6 Bxc3+ 16. bxc3 Bb7 { Black gets some practical compensation, but still, far from equalizing. } 17. Bg2 O-O-O 18. Qh5 Qb5 19. Qe2 $14 { With a safe endgame with an extra pawn. } *


