1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f4 e5 7. Nb3 Qc7 { Very serious try. } 8. Be2! { The best
chance. } { [%csl Be2][%cal Ge1g1,Gg2g4] } 8... Nbd7 { Black's best continuation. } { [%csl Bd7] } (8... Be6 { Quite a shaky approach for Black. } 9. g4 { Already looks
scary for Black. } { [%cal Gf4f5,Gg4g5] } 9... exf4 { Most common. } 10. Bxf4 h6 { Black really must prevent g4-g5 here. } { [%cal Rg4g5] } 11. Qd2 Nc6 12. O-O-O O-O-O 13. h4 $16 { With a pleasant advantage for White. A possible plan is Rh1-h3 with the idea Nc3-d5 and Rh3-c3. } { [%cal Bh3c3,Bh1h3,Bc3d5] }) (8... b5 { Most common continuation. } 9. O-O Bb7 10. a4! { We switch to positional chess. } { [%csl Ba4] } 10... b4 11. Nd5 Nxd5 12. exd5 Nd7 13. c4 { Or c2-c3,
it's the same. } 13... bxc3 14. bxc3 { Of course, taking on c3 is too risky here. } { [%cal Rc7c3,Bc1d2] } 14... Be7 15. c4 O-O 16. fxe5 $146 { [%cal Rf6e5] } 16... Nxe5 17. Be3 Rae8 18. Rc1 $14 { White is positionally better, a4-a5 next, and Black simply lacks counterplay. } { [%csl Rb7][%cal Ga4a5] }) 9. O-O { We continue developing. } 9... Be7 { The best. } 10. g4! $146 { We must play concretely to achieve anything. } { [%csl Bg4] } 10... Nc5 { Human move. } (10... Qc6! { Critical move. } { [%csl Bc6,Re4] } 11. Bf3 { Defending e4. } 11... h6 { [%csl Yc6,Yf3][%cal Rc3d5] } 12. Kh1!? $132 { I recommend this
quiet move now. It's definitely useful, and asks Black what they want to do now. After all, Black still needs to develop. } { [%csl Bh1] }) 11. Bf3 { We
simply defend e4. } 11... Nxb3 12. axb3 exf4 13. Bxf4 Be6 { Looks like a very logical setup for Black. } 14. g5 Nd7 15. Nd5 { Seizing the bishop
pair. } 15... Bxd5 16. Qxd5 Ne5 17. Bh5 { Creating problems on f7, but Black has } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Gf4e5] } 17... O-O! { And the problem is that taking on
f7 is too risky and gives nothing, but we have } 18. c3 $14 { And the bishop pair gives us some advantage and a very playable
position. } { [%csl Bf4,Bh5] } *


