1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f4 e5 7. Nb3 Nc6 { This is a very serious move we recommended in our course "Najdorf
for Black". } 8. f5!? { The only interesting approach. } { [%csl Bf5] } 8... d5 { What we offered for Black, and I think this is the most critical
move. } (8... Be7 { Black's most common continuation. } 9. Bd3 { Logical development. } 9... O-O 10. Be3 { Most flexible option. } 10... a5!! { The best option. } { [%csl Ba5][%cal Rd1d2,Re1c1,Ya2a4,Yc6b4] } 11. a3 { Very logical and useful reply. } { [%csl Ga3][%cal Rc6b4,Ra4a3] } 11... a4 12. Nd2 g6! { Black must do something against the f5-pawn. } { [%cal Gg6f5] } 13. Bh6 Re8 14. g4 { We support f5. } { [%csl Gf5] } 14... Nd4 15. O-O $132 { With a balanced game, but I prefer White, as it seems easier to play. } { [%cal Gc3d5,Gg1h1] }) 9. Bg5 { Only chance. } 9... Nb4 10. a3 d4 { This is a very
interesting approach from Black and is objectively very sound. } 11. axb4 { The critical test. } (11. Nb1 { Positional way for a long game. } 11... Nc6 12. N1d2 $132 { Bd3-O-O next, keeping pieces on the board and playing for the kingside is the idea. } { [%cal Gf1d3,Ge1g1] }) 11... Bxb4 12. Bxf6 Qxf6 13. Bb5+! { Only move to keep up the fire. } { [%csl Gb5] } 13... Ke7 14. Qd3!? { Interesting, trying to avoid Be6. } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Rc8e6] } 14... Rd8! { Most accurate reply. } { [%csl Bd8] } (14... dxc3 { Very logical to start with, but here we get a game. } 15. bxc3 Rd8 16. Qe3 Bd6 17. Bc4 Bc7 { Very logical play by both sides. } 18. Bd5 { Against b5. } { [%cal Rb7b5] } 18... Bb6 19. Qg3 Bd7 { Logical idea, but after } { [%cal Gd7c6] } 20. Bxb7 Ra7 21. Bd5 $36 { We take the pawn, and Black must prove the compensation here. } { [%cal Gb3d2,Gd2c4,Bc3c4] }) 15. O-O-O axb5 16. Qxb5 Bxc3 17. bxc3 $132 { Of course, the position is close to full equality, but I think White has no risk and can get some practical chances. } *


