1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f4 e6 { Second most common and very playable line. } 7. Qf3!? { My recommendation, we simply want to develop the queenside, and keep the g4 resource in reserve. } { [%cal Gc1e3,Ge1c1,Bg2g4] } 7... Qb6 { Main line. } (7... Qc7?! { Popular reply, with 750 games, but quite dubious. } 8. g4! { The inclusion is definitely in White's favor. } { [%csl Yc7,Gf3] } 8... Nc6 9. Nxc6! { We must play concretely. } { [%csl Gc6] } 9... bxc6 { [%cal Gd6d5] } 10. g5 Nd7 11. Bd2!? { I like this
semi-prophylactic move. } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gd2c3] } 11... d5 12. h4 { Bb4 makes no sense now. } { [%cal Rf8b4] } 12... d4!? $146 { Engine's recommendation. } 13. Na4 c5 14. b3! $16 { Typical defensive setup, and White has a safe positional advantage. } { [%csl Gb3][%cal Ga4b2,Gh4h5] }) 8. a3!? { Overall useful move, preventing any b5-b4 ideas, and of course, Black cannot just grab the knight here. } { [%csl Ba3][%cal Yb7b5,Yb5b4,Rb6d4,Gc1e3] } 8... Nc6 { Most
common by far. } (8... Nbd7?! { tried by Kasparov a few times. } 9. Be2! $146 { [%csl Be2] } 9... Be7 10. Bd2 $16 { Long castling next,
with a large advantage. } { [%cal Ge1c1] }) 9. Nxc6! { We take of course. } 9... bxc6 { Necessary. } 10. e5! { That's another reason why I like 8.a3, in
the main line we reach our typical play against Black's structure. } { [%csl Ge5] } 10... Nd7 { Best. } 11. exd6 Bxd6 12. Qg3! $146 { Annoying tempo. } { [%csl Rg7] } 12... Be7! { Engine's try. } (12... O-O 13. Ne4 Be7 14. Bd2 $16 { White's attack is quite dangerous. } { [%csl Rg8][%cal Gd2c3,Gf1d3] }) 13. Qxg7 Bh4+! { The point, to weaken some squares. } 14. g3 Bf6 15. Qh6 Bxc3+ 16. bxc3 Bb7 { Black gets some practical compensation, but still, far from
equalizing. } 17. Bg2 O-O-O 18. Qh5 Qb5 19. Qe2 $14 { With a safe endgame with an extra pawn. } *


