1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f4 Qc7 { third most common move, Black stops e5 } 7. f5!? { Very
rare and interesting approach to quickly play with g4. } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bg2g4] } 7... e5 { most common, to force White to take } { [%cal Rf5e6] } (7... Nc6 { tried by GM
Korobov, but we can highlight the drawback with: } 8. Bg5! { Good reply, and Black doesn't have Nbd7. } { [%csl Yc6,Rf6][%cal Gg5f6,Gc3d5] } 8... Qb6 { best } 9. Nxc6 bxc6! { necessary } 10. Qd2 { Mazur-Korobov, 2022. } 10... e6 11. O-O-O!? $146 { Improvement over the game. } { [%csl Bc1] } 11... Rb8 12. b3 d5 { Maybe this is what White was scared of, but it can be met with: } 13. Qe1!? $14 { typical antidote } { [%csl Re8][%cal Ge1e8] }) 8. fxe6 fxe6 9. Be2!? { most promising reply } { [%cal Ge2h5] } 9... Be7 { Black's best. } 10. Bh5+! { White's idea, Black can't fianchetto the bishop
anymore. } { [%cal Rf8g7] } 10... g6 11. Be2 { Now the dark squares on the kingside are forever weak. } { [%csl Yg7,Yh6] } 11... O-O { Paravyan-Bocharov, 2020. } (11... e5 $146 { human attempt, to develop with Be6 next } 12. Nb3 Be6 13. Bh6 { We can stop castling for now. } { [%cal Re8g8] } 13... Nbd7 14. O-O Bf8!? { most
logical } 15. Bg5 { tempo } { [%csl Rf6] } 15... Be7! { Black has nothing better than to repeat. } { [%cal Bg5h6] } 16. a4!? { But we can play
for more. } { [%cal Ga4a5] } 16... O-O 17. a5 $36 { Objectively normal for Black, but still this outcome is okay, no theory, playable
position, and long-term ideas due to the weak dark squares. } { [%csl Yg7,Yh6][%cal Gd1d2] }) 12. Bh6 Rf7 13. Qd3 $14 { long castle next and White is slightly
better } { [%cal Ge1c1] } *


