1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f4 Nc6 { Rare system, usually played by GMs ready for a fight. } 7. Nxc6 { My
recommendation, we transform the structure. } 7... bxc6 8. e5! { The idea is to break Black's chain, leaving
the damaged a6-c6 pawns, and we are not scared to do it even at the cost of a pawn. } { [%csl Ra6,Rc6,Bf8][%cal Rd6e5,Gf4e5] } 8... Nd7! { Necessary. } 9. Bc4 { Most testing, Black has
tried a few moves: } 9... Nb6 { Most common and best, to play d5. } { [%csl Rc4][%cal Gd6d5] } (9... dxe5?! { Quite bad. } 10. Qf3! $146 { Strongest move, the idea is to castle long fast. } { [%cal Gc1d2,Ge1c1] } 10... e6! { Necessary. } 11. Bd2 Be7 12. O-O-O $14 { Huge
development lead in return for one pawn. } { [%csl Gc3,Gc4,Gd2,Gf3] }) (9... e6 { Playable, but leads to a comfortable structure. } 10. exd6 Nb6! { Black's idea. } 11. Qd3!? $146 { Interesting novelty, we don't waste time on development. } { [%cal Gc1e3] } 11... Nxc4 12. Qxc4 Qxd6 13. Be3 Rb8 14. b3 $14 { Shouldn't be so bad for Black, but it's still not full equality. } { [%csl Ra6,Rc6][%cal Ga1d1,Ge1g1,Rd6b4,Gc4d3] }) 10. Qf3!? $146 { I like this move order, making sure that d5 will be played. } { [%csl Rc6][%cal Rb6c4,Gf3c6] } 10... d5 11. Bd3 e6 12. O-O { We enter this
interesting structure. } { [%csl Yb6][%cal Gd7c5] } 12... Be7 13. b3!? { Useful prophylaxis against c5-c4, and supporting the future c2-c4. } { [%cal Yc6c5,Yc5c4,Gc2c4] } 13... O-O 14. Ne2 c5 15. Ng3 { White is ready for f5. } { [%cal Bf4f5] } 15... f5! { Or f6 is necessary. } 16. exf6 Bxf6 17. Rb1 { It's riskier for Black here, as one
bad move can mean game over. } 17... g6!? { Probably most sound. } 18. Be3 Qc7 19. c4 $14 { Qf2 next, to harass the
structure more, and White is definitely for choice. } { [%csl Yc5,Yd5][%cal Gf3f2,Rd5d4] } *


